WEBVTT
TITLE CPU performance: the equation, CPI, MIPS and Amdahl's law — GATE Visual Explainer
NOTE Timings are estimated from the lesson timeline (speaking-rate based), not measured from recorded audio.

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:07.500
Every program's running time is the product of three numbers: instructions, cycles per instruction, and the clock period.

2
00:00:07.500 --> 00:00:15.000
Instruction count comes from the program, the compiler and the instruction set.

3
00:00:15.000 --> 00:00:22.500
CPI is the average number of clock cycles one instruction takes.

4
00:00:22.500 --> 00:00:30.000
The clock period is the time of one cycle, so 2 GHz means 0.5 nanoseconds. Multiply the three and you have seconds.

5
00:00:30.000 --> 00:00:37.000
A 2 GHz clock ticks two billion times a second, so one cycle lasts half a nanosecond.

6
00:00:37.000 --> 00:00:44.000
Rate and period are reciprocals: the period in nanoseconds is 1000 divided by the rate in megahertz.

7
00:00:44.000 --> 00:00:51.000
So doubling the rate halves the period, and that alone halves the time, if the other two numbers stay put.

8
00:00:51.000 --> 00:00:58.000
That condition is the whole difficulty of the subject: the three dials are not independent.

9
00:00:58.000 --> 00:01:05.000
Instructions are not equal: an ALU add may take one cycle and a floating-point multiply five.

10
00:01:05.000 --> 00:01:12.000
Weight each class by its share of the executed instructions: 50% at 1 cycle, 25% at 2, 15% at 3, 10% at 5.

11
00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:19.000
The weighted sum is 0.5 + 0.5 + 0.45 + 0.5 = 1.95 cycles per instruction.

12
00:01:19.000 --> 00:01:26.000
That is the only correct average. Averaging the four CPIs to 2.75 ignores how often each class runs.

13
00:01:26.000 --> 00:01:33.000
One million instructions at 1.95 cycles each is 1.95 million cycles, and at 2 GHz that is 0.975 milliseconds.

14
00:01:33.000 --> 00:01:39.600
Machine A runs at 2 GHz with the CPI of 1.95 just computed: 0.975 milliseconds.

15
00:01:39.600 --> 00:01:46.200
Machine B has a faster clock, 3 GHz, but its compiler emits 30% more instructions and its CPI is 2.4.

16
00:01:46.200 --> 00:01:52.800
Machine B needs 1.04 milliseconds, so the faster clock lost: 50% more frequency, 6.7% longer run.

17
00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:59.400
So 'which is faster' is never answered by the clock rate. Compute the product, or do not answer.

18
00:01:59.400 --> 00:02:06.000
Both machines are checked in code: the cycle counts, the times, the ratio, and the direction of the conclusion.

19
00:02:06.000 --> 00:02:12.000
MIPS is millions of instructions per second: the instruction count divided by the time, in millions.

20
00:02:12.000 --> 00:02:18.000
Machine A reads 1026 MIPS and machine B reads 1250 MIPS. B wins on MIPS and loses on time.

21
00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:24.000
First failure: MIPS ignores what an instruction does. Powerful instructions mean fewer of them per second and a shorter run.

22
00:02:24.000 --> 00:02:30.000
Second failure: MIPS varies between programs on the same machine, because CPI varies with the mix.

23
00:02:30.000 --> 00:02:36.000
Third failure: MIPS can move opposite to performance, which is exactly what A and B did.

24
00:02:36.000 --> 00:02:42.000
Averaging MIPS across programs needs the harmonic mean, not the arithmetic mean, because a rate is being averaged.

25
00:02:42.000 --> 00:02:49.000
Speedup is the old time divided by the new time: a ratio of times, never a ratio of frequencies.

26
00:02:49.000 --> 00:02:56.000
A machine that runs 20% faster has speedup 1.2, so its time is 0.833 of the old, a 16.7% reduction and not 20%.

27
00:02:56.000 --> 00:03:03.000
A time cut by 20% is a speedup of 1.25, because one divided by 0.8 is 1.25.

28
00:03:03.000 --> 00:03:10.000
Write down which of the two the question gave you before doing any arithmetic.

29
00:03:10.000 --> 00:03:17.000
Suppose 60% of the time is spent in the part you can speed up, and you make that part four times faster.

30
00:03:17.000 --> 00:03:24.000
The other 40% is untouched. The new time is 0.4 + 0.6/4 = 0.55 of the old, so the speedup is 1.818.

31
00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:31.000
Push the improved part to infinity and the speedup stops at 1/0.4 = 2.5. That is the ceiling.

32
00:03:31.000 --> 00:03:38.000
The formula is one over ((1 - p) + p/s): the unimproved fraction plus the improved fraction divided by its speedup.

33
00:03:38.000 --> 00:03:45.000
p is the fraction of time, not of code and not of instructions.

34
00:03:45.000 --> 00:03:52.500
Questions often give the target overall speedup and ask for the local one. With 90% of the time improvable and a target of 2, solve 2 = 1/(0.1 + 0.9/s).

35
00:03:52.500 --> 00:04:00.000
That gives s = 2.25: the improved part must be more than twice as fast, not twice.

36
00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:07.500
And if the target exceeds 1/(1-p), no speedup achieves it. With p = 0.6 the ceiling is 2.5, so a target of 3 is impossible.

37
00:04:07.500 --> 00:04:15.000
Check feasibility first, then solve. That order saves the algebra and catches the impossible question.

38
00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:21.600
A real CPI is the ideal CPI plus the cycles lost waiting. Memory stalls per instruction are the miss rate times the miss penalty in cycles.

39
00:04:21.600 --> 00:04:28.200
Base CPI 1.2, miss rate 3%, penalty 60 cycles: 1.8 stall cycles per instruction, so the CPI is 3.0.

40
00:04:28.200 --> 00:04:34.800
A perfect cache would give CPI 1.2, and the time would fall from 1.5 to 0.6 milliseconds, a speedup of 2.5.

41
00:04:34.800 --> 00:04:41.400
Turned around: to reach CPI 2.0 the miss rate must fall to 1.33%. That is the arithmetic a cache question is really asking.

42
00:04:41.400 --> 00:04:48.000
This is the same equation as before with one number replaced. Nothing new is introduced, only measured.

43
00:04:48.000 --> 00:04:55.000
A k-stage pipeline has an ideal speedup of k, because it issues one instruction per cycle instead of one per k cycles.

44
00:04:55.000 --> 00:05:02.000
Stalls raise the CPI: five stages with 0.2 stall cycles per instruction give CPI 1.2 and a speedup near 5/1.2 = 4.17.

45
00:05:02.000 --> 00:05:09.000
Doubling the clock rate while doubling the CPI leaves the time unchanged: speedup 1, for twice the frequency.

46
00:05:09.000 --> 00:05:16.000
So the three dials trade against each other: deeper pipelining buys frequency and spends CPI.

47
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:22.000
Step one: write the three quantities the question gives, with units. Frequency is not time, so convert it.

48
00:05:22.000 --> 00:05:28.000
Step two: if the question gives a mix, weight the CPIs. If it gives total cycles, divide by the instruction count.

49
00:05:28.000 --> 00:05:34.000
Step three: multiply, and keep the powers of ten straight. A nanosecond is 10^-9 seconds and a millisecond is 10^-3.

50
00:05:34.000 --> 00:05:40.000
Step four: answer the number that was asked for. Execution time, CPI, MIPS and speedup are four different answers.

51
00:05:40.000 --> 00:05:46.000
Check the answer against the bounds: a speedup cannot exceed the ceiling, and a CPI cannot be below one for a scalar pipeline.

52
00:05:46.000 --> 00:05:52.500
One equation, three dials: instructions, cycles per instruction, clock period.

53
00:05:52.500 --> 00:05:59.000
Speedup is a ratio of times. Amdahl caps it at one over the unimproved fraction.

54
00:05:59.000 --> 00:06:05.500
MIPS is not a measure of speed. It measures instruction throughput under fixed program, ISA and compiler.

55
00:06:05.500 --> 00:06:12.000
Everything else in the subject, cache and pipeline and compiler, is an argument about which dial it moves.

